<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #333;">___</span> – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pros and Cons</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a routine task. Interactive: Negotiate solutions to challenges in group work.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain routine tech-free activities by responding to peer questions (e.g., "I like to read when I'm not using my phone."). Can ask and answer questions about daily tech-free habits (e.g., "I can read more when I am not using my computer."). Can negotiate solutions to group challenges related to technology use by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "How can we reduce screen time?" "We could set tech-free hours.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Tech-Free Habits Chain Game." The teacher begins by saying, "I like to read when I'm not using my phone." The next student repeats the sentence and adds their own tech-free activity. Students take turns adding a new activity, building a chain. This activates prior knowledge of daily tech-free habits and introduces the speaking task.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences for giving opinions and suggestions related to technology's pros and cons, using modals and comparatives. For example, "I think technology is helpful because it makes learning easier." (pronunciation of /ð/ in "the" and /θ/ in "think"). "You could try to reduce screen time by disconnecting after 8 PM." (pronunciation of /d/ in "disconnected"). The teacher demonstrates how to discuss pros and cons and offer suggestions in a clear, audible voice.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target language.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You should save more time for friends,' am I giving an order or a suggestion?" (Expected answer: "A suggestion.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'more difficult' comparing one thing or two things?" (Expected answer: "Two things.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (approx. 1.5 minutes) between two 8th-grade friends discussing a new app or device (e.g., a "smartwatch") and its pros and cons. The dialogue uses vocabulary such as "device," "social media," "helpful," "distracted," "reduce," "interact," and grammar points like modals ("can," "could," "should") and comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"). Register: informal conversation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret and comprehend main ideas and key details from short, recorded dialogues about daily routines and personal preferences related to technology.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are the two friends discussing?" "Do they agree about the new device?" (Students raise hands or give short oral responses).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and decide if statements related to the dialogue are true or false, responding orally: e.g., "The friends think social media makes interaction easier." "One friend suggests they should use the device less." (Teacher asks students to say "True" or "False").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-10 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modals ("can," "could," "should") for suggestions and ability, comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), and present perfect ("since," "for"). They integrate vocabulary: "technology," "cellphone," "internet," "device," "social media," "solution," "benefits," "use," "reduce," "disconnect," "interact," "save," "helpful," "meaningful," "connected," "relaxed," "distracted," "focused." Focus on pronunciation of /θ/ and /ð/ and past tense -ed endings (/d/, /ɪd/, /t/).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Builders (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and prompts on the board/projector. Students complete them orally, focusing on modals and comparatives. Example prompts: "Using a cellphone for hours ______ be distracting." (can/could) "Reading a book is ______ relaxing than scrolling." (more) "You ______ reduce screen time." (should). The teacher ensures correct pronunciation of words like "mouth" (n.) vs "mouth" (v.) if they arise during sentence formation, distinguishing /θ/ and /ð/ sounds.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Since or For" Statements (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher displays pictures or prompts of actions and states of being (e.g., "playing outside," "feeling relaxed," "studying"). Students create sentences using the present perfect with "since" or "for" to explain changes in habits due to technology use or non-use. Example: "I have felt more relaxed since I disconnected from social media at night." "I have used my device for five hours today." They also practice "saved" (/d/), "disconnected" (/ɪd/), and "liked" (/t/) endings when describing activities.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Pros and Cons Discussion:</b> Students, in pairs, discuss one specific piece of technology (e.g., "social media," "video games," "the internet"). They take turns giving opinions and discussing its pros and cons, offering suggestions for responsible use. They must use phrases like: "I think [technology] is useful because...", "Digital technology makes [X] faster and easier, but it can be distracting," and "You could try using less screen time by...".</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion & Negotiation (4-5 students per group). Students will participate in a guided classroom debate, proposing solutions for technology overuse.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents a challenge: "Our group spends too much time on our phones/devices and it's making us distracted during school and less connected with friends and family."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, in their groups, must discuss the problem and collectively negotiate at least three practical solutions to reduce screen time. They need to agree on these solutions as a group.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should use target language to: give opinions ("I think social media is a big problem because it's so distracting."), discuss pros and cons (of proposed solutions), and offer suggestions ("We could set tech-free hours every evening," "You should try to disconnect your device an hour before bed."). They also practice asking and answering questions about daily tech-free habits and negotiate solutions to group challenges.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The groups will briefly present their negotiated solutions orally to the class, explaining the benefits.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, negotiation of solutions, and use of target grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect) and vocabulary in a realistic context. Students actively participate in proposing and evaluating solutions, practicing their speaking skills.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will present their group's negotiated solutions to the class (brief oral report). The teacher will use an observation checklist to assess students' active participation and use of target language during the group discussion and presentation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear production of sounds, especially /θ/, /ð/, and -ed verb endings.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses words like "reduce," "disconnect," "solution," "distracted," "focused."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Explains tech-free activities and solutions clearly.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction and Negotiation:</b> Asks and answers questions effectively, offers suggestions, and negotiates solutions.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of modals ("can," "could," "should"), comparatives, and present perfect "since/for."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written reflection before leaving.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned about discussing technology's pros and cons today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one suggestion I could give to a friend who uses too much social media?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel explaining my tech-free habits and offering suggestions to reduce screen time?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities was most challenging for me, and why?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., student engagement, use of target language, interaction levels).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their speaking fluency and accuracy in discussing solutions and pros/cons?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better support students in pronouncing minimal pairs /θ/-/ð/ and past tense endings?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the ideas and vocabulary generated in today's discussions to write short paragraphs or an essay about the pros and cons of technology and propose solutions for its responsible use.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>